<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">Section 4.7 Exercises</heading>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, find all critical points.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="310" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{4} + y^{4} - 16xy$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="311" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 15x^{3} - 3xy + 15y^{3}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 2 3 , 4 ) ( 2 3 , 4 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="312" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="313" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = -x^{2} - 5y^{2} + 8x - 10y - 13$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(0,0)(0,0)$$ $$(115,115)(115,115)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, find the critical points of the function by using algebraic techniques (completing the square) or by examining the form of the equation. Verify your results using the partial derivatives test.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="314" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2x - 6y + 6$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="315" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} + 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Maximum at $$(4,-1,8)(4,-1,8)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="316" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = -x^{3} + 4xy - 2y^{2} + 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="317" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2}y^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Relative minimum at $$(0,0,1)(0,0,1)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use the second derivative test to classify any critical points and determine whether each critical point is a maximum, minimum, saddle point, or none of these.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="318" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} - 6x + y^{2} + 4y - 8$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="319" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 2xy + 3x + 4y$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The second derivative test fails. Since $$x2y2&gt;0x2y2&gt;0$$ for all x and y different from zero, and $$x2y2=0x2y2=0$$ when either x or y equals zero (or both), then the absolute minimum occurs at $$(0,0).(0,0).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="320" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 8xy(x + y) + 7$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="321" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + 4xy + y^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$f(-2,-32)=-6f(-2,-32)=-6$$ is a saddle point.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="322" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{3} + y^{3} - 300x - 75y - 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="323" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 9 - x^{4}y^{4}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$f(0,0)=0;f(0,0)=0;$$ $$(0,0,0)(0,0,0)$$ is a saddle point.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="324" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 7x^{2}y + 9xy^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="325" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 3x^{2} - 2xy + y^{2} - 8y$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The only critical point(s) are where both $$fx=0fx=0$$ and $$fy=0fy=0$$ and that is $$f(0, 0)=9f(0, 0)=9$$ . However, since $$fxxfxy-fxy2=0fxxfxy-fxy2=0$$ at the critical point, the Second Derivative Test fails. By graphing, we can see that this point is a local maximum.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="326" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 3x^{2} + 2xy + y^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="327" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = y^{2} + xy + 3y + 2x + 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Relative minimum located at $$(2,6).(2,6).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="328" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + xy + y^{2} - 3x$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="329" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + 2y^{2} - x^{2}y$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(1,-2)(1,-2)$$ is a saddle point.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="330" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + y - e^{y}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="331" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = e^{-(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2x)}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(2,1)(2,1)$$ and $$(-2,1)(-2,1)$$ are saddle points; $$(0,0)(0,0)$$ is a relative minimum.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="332" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + xy + y^{2} - x - y + 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="333" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + 10xy + y^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(-1,0)(-1,0)$$ is a relative maximum.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="334" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = -x^{2} - 5y^{2} + 10x - 30y - 62$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="335" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 120x + 120y - xy - x^{2} - y^{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(0,0)(0,0)$$ is a saddle point.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="336" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 2x^{2} + 2xy + y^{2} + 2x - 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="337" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x^{2} + x - 3xy + y^{3} - 5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The relative maximum is at $$(40,40).(40,40).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="338" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = 2xye^{-x^{2} - y^{2}}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="339" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = ye^{x} - e^{y}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(14,12)(14,12)$$ is a saddle point and $$(1,1)(1,1)$$ is the relative minimum.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="340" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = x \sin (y)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, determine the extreme values and the saddle points. Use a CAS to graph the function.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="341" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$f(x,y) = \sin (x)\sin (y),x \in \left( {0,2\pi} \right),y \in \left( {0,2\pi} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>A saddle point is located at $$(0,0).(0,0).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="342" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$R.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="343" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$f(x,y) = xy - x - 3y;$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>There is a saddle point at $$(\pi ,\pi ),(\pi ,\pi ),$$ local maxima at $$(\pi 2,\pi 2)and(3\pi 2,3\pi 2),(\pi 2,\pi 2)and(3\pi 2,3\pi 2),$$ and local minima at $$(\pi 2,3\pi 2)and(3\pi 2,\pi 2).(\pi 2,3\pi 2)and(3\pi 2,\pi 2).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>Find the absolute extrema of the given function on the indicated closed and bounded set $$R$$</paragraph>
    <exercise number="344" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {0,0} \right),\left( {0,4} \right), \text{and} \left( {5,0} \right).$$ $$f(x,y) = x^{2} + y^{2} - 2y + 1$$ is the triangular region with vertices $$R = \left\{ {(x,y)\left| {x^{2} + y^{2} \leq 4} \right.} \right\}.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="345" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of $$f(x,y) = x^{3} - 3xy - y^{3}$$ on the region $$R = \left\{ (x,y): -2 \leq x \leq 2,-2 \leq y \leq 2 \right\}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$(0,1,0)(0,1,0)$$ is the absolute minimum and $$(0,-2,9)(0,-2,9)$$ is the absolute maximum.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="346" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x,y) = \frac{-2y}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}$$ on $$R = \left\{ {(x,y): x^{2} + y^{2} \leq 4} \right\}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="347" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$27,$$ on $$x^{2} - yz = 5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>There is an absolute minimum at $$(0,1,-1)(0,1,-1)$$ and an absolute maximum at $$(0,-1,1).(0,-1,1).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="348" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find three positive numbers the sum of which is $$x + y + z = 1.$$ such that the sum of their squares is as small as possible.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="349" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the points on the surface $$108$$ that are closest to the origin.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 5 , 0 , 0 ) , ( - 5 , 0 , 0 ) ( 5 , 0 , 0 ) , ( - 5 , 0 , 0 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="350" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the maximum volume of a rectangular box with three faces in the coordinate planes and a vertex in the first octant on the plane $$4$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="351" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The sum of the length and the girth (perimeter of a cross-section) of a package carried by a delivery service cannot exceed $$f(x,y) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 10$$ in. Find the dimensions of the rectangular package of largest volume that can be sent.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$18 by 36 by 18 in . 18 by 36 by 18 in .$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="352" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A cardboard box without a lid is to be made with a volume of $$x + 2y - z = 0.$$ ft3. Find the dimensions of the box that requires the least amount of cardboard.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="353" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the point on the surface $$2x - y + 2z = 16$$ nearest the plane $$x$$ Identify the point on the plane.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 1 2 , 1 , 45 4 ) ( 1 2 , 1 , 45 4 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="354" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the point in the plane $$y$$ that is closest to the origin.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="355" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A company manufactures two types of athletic shoes: jogging shoes and cross-trainers. The total revenue from $$R(x,y) = -5x^{2} - 8y^{2} - 2xy + 42x + 102y,$$ jogging shoes and $$x$$ cross-trainers is given by $$y$$ where $$96$$ and $$120$$ are in thousands of units. Find the values of x and y to maximize the total revenue.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$x=3x=3$$ and $$y=6y=6$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="356" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A shipping company handles rectangular boxes provided the sum of the length, width, and height of the box does not exceed $$9696$$ in. Find the dimensions of the box that meets this condition and has the largest volume.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="357" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the maximum volume of a cylindrical soda can such that the sum of its height and circumference is $$120120$$ cm.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$V=64,000\pi \approx 20,372V=64,000\pi \approx 20,372$$ cm3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
